INT. STALLED ELEVATOR, LAW FIRM TOWER - NIGHT

A brushed steel box. Four people. A dim emergency light. The floor indicator reads 14 and does not change.

MARGUERITE DUVAL, 50s, opposing counsel, in a navy suit, holds a briefcase to her chest. ROLAND CHASE, 40s, lead attorney, loosens his tie. ELSPETH, 30s, a junior associate, sits on the floor with her laptop. JAMAL, 50s, the building's maintenance man, presses the intercom.

MARGUERITE
Roland, I'm not signing anything in an elevator.

ROLAND
You don't have a choice.

MARGUERITE
I always have a choice.

ROLAND
Name one.

MARGUERITE
I can wait.

ROLAND
For what?

MARGUERITE
For you to run out of air.

ELSPETH
There's plenty of air.

JAMAL
There's plenty of air. The cables are the problem.

ROLAND
Mr. Jamal, how long?

JAMAL
Hour. Maybe two.

MARGUERITE
Two hours?

ELSPETH
It's in the contract, Marguerite.

MARGUERITE
The elevator?

ELSPETH
The deadline. Midnight.

MARGUERITE
(checking watch)
It's eleven-twenty.

ROLAND
You see. You see.

MARGUERITE
I see a man who parked his deal in an elevator.

JAMAL
(into intercom)
Dispatch, we've got a stuck car on fourteen. Four occupants. Two lawyers, one associate, one me.

The intercom crackles. Jamal listens, nods.

ELSPETH
What did they say?

JAMAL
Twenty minutes. They always say twenty minutes.

ROLAND
Elspeth, print the settlement.

ELSPETH
There's no printer.

ROLAND
Then write it by hand.

ELSPETH
In what?

ROLAND
Anything.

Elspeth opens her laptop. Marguerite snatches it, and Roland lunges, and the laptop tumbles between them, bounces off the wall, and slides to Jamal's feet. He picks it up and holds it carefully, like a baby.

JAMAL
Folks, I got a rule in my elevators.

MARGUERITE
(overlapping)
This isn't your elevator --

ROLAND
(overlapping)
Give it to me --

ELSPETH
(overlapping)
It's mine, actually --

JAMAL
No fighting. No yelling. No lawyering.

The three of them stop. The emergency light flickers. A groan from the cables above, deep and long.

The car drops two inches. Everyone grabs a rail. Marguerite drops her briefcase. Roland sits down hard. Elspeth screams, once, short and sharp. Jamal alone remains standing.

JAMAL (CONT'D)
That's the cable settling.

MARGUERITE
(whispering)
That's the cable settling?

JAMAL
That's what it sounds like when it's settling.

ELSPETH
And when it's not?

JAMAL
You won't have to ask.

Silence. Roland retrieves a pen from his pocket. Marguerite, after a moment, holds out her hand. He gives it to her.

She opens her briefcase, draws a thick document, and signs the last page on the wall of the elevator. The ink bleeds into the steel.

MARGUERITE
Witness, Mr. Jamal.

JAMAL
I witness.

He signs. Hands the pen back. The car shudders. The indicator light flickers. 14 becomes 13.
